#ident	"@(#)gen++:d.pkg/metrics/README	1.3"

DESCRIPTION:
This directory contains a gen++ tool to calculate the 6 OO metrics
designed by S. Chidamber and C. Kemerer at MIT.  A complete description
of the metrics and their theoretical basis can be found in "A Metrics
Suite for Object Oriented Design", S Chidamber, C Kemerer, IEEE, 1994.
Briefly, the metrics include
  WMC (weighted methods per class) -- in this case,
        a count of the number of methods in each class
  DIT (depth of inheritance tree) -- the maximum depth of
        the inheritance graph for each class
  NOC (number of children) -- the number of direct descendents
        for each class
  CBO (coupling between object classes) -- where a class is
        coupled to another if one uses methods or instance
        variables of the other
  RFC (response for a class) -- set of methods that can po-
        tentially be executed in response to a message received
        by an object of that class.  In this case it is the set
        of functions directly invoked by member functions of a
        class.
  LCOM (lack of cohesion in methods) -- count of the number of
        methods pairs without shared instance variables minus
        the number with shared instance variables.
The metrics tool in this package is a gen++ analyzer that
generates data for all 6 metrics for each file in an application.
This data is then combined and processed by the accompanying scripts
to produce 5 files (described below).  These files contain tables
that can be fed through the S package to obtain histograms for the
various metrics.

FILES:
You should have received the following files:
	README
	metrics.query -- gen++ query
	count, noc -- small awk scripts
	LCOM -- C++ program to process LCOM data.

Metrics.query processes data for all 6 metrics.
For each c++ file x.c, 5 files are produced:
	x.M == WMC and DIT data; each line==classname;WMC;DIT
	x.N == NOC data; each line==class1;class2 where class2
		is derived form class1
	x.O == CBO data; each line==class1;class2 where class1
		couples with class2
	x.P == RFC data; each line==class;method where method
		is a function in the response set of class
	x.L == LCOM data; each line==C;M;V where C is a class
		containing method M and V is an instance variable
		in C referenced by M
Notes:
 * In 2 of the metrics (CBO and RFC), <global> refers to "global scope",
   which is treated as a special superclass.  Its methods are all global
   functions.  (This may be deleted in subsequent releases.)
   The presence of <global> may inflate the data for CBO and RFC slightly.
   It is included primarily to obtain information on use of global names.
 * Since virtual functions can only be identified at runtime, RFC and CBO
   calculations only count the "static name" of virtual functions called.
   I.e., if a virtual function call f() is encountered, metrics.query
   will count baseclass::f() as if it were not virtual.  There is no way
   to determine the identity of virtual functions at compile time.
 * To ensure uniqueness of various types of names (functions, global, local
   and nested classes and instantiated templates, etc), mangled names are
   used in the generated tables.
 * To ensure uniqueness of file static names, static names are printed
   with the C file name appended.

TO PROCESS METRICS:
 * Build analyzer from metrics.query.
 * Build application using the analyzer built from metrics.query.
   This is done by editing QCC to use this analyzer then building
   your application with CC=QCC.
 * Execute the following in the order shown.  Note: Be sure to include
   all the metrics data files from all relevant directories.
	cat *.M | sort -u >mmm
	cat *.N | sort -u | count >nn0; noc >nnn
	cat *.O | sort -u | count >ooo
	cat *.P | sort -u | count >ppp
	cat *.L | sort -u | sed -e 's/ /-/g' | LCOM >lll
Notes:
 * mmm will contain WMC and DIT data for all classes or structs,
   not including <global>
 * nn0 will include NOC data for all classes that have children.
   For a complete histogram, it is necessary to add the names of
   all remaining classes.  This is done using the noc script
   which merges data from mmm and nn0.
 * ooo contains CBO info for classes, including <global>
 * ppp contains RFC info for classes that have member
   functions, including <global>
 * lll contains LCOM info for classes with member functions.

COMMENTS WELCOME!
